.TH @MANPAGE_NAME@ 1 2017-01-12 "@FREERDP_VERSION_FULL@" "FreeRDP"
.SH NAME
@MANPAGE_NAME@ \- A utility for sharing a X display via RDP.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @MANPAGE_NAME@
[\fB/port:\fP\fI<port number>\fP]
[\fB/ipc-socket:\fP\fI<ipc-socket>\fP]
[\fB/monitors:\fP\fI<0,1,2,...>\fP]
[\fB/subsystem:\fP\fI<name>[,<name>...]\fP]
[\fB/rect:\fP\fI<x,y,w,h>\fP]
[\fB/codec:\fP\fI<codec>:<key>:<value>[,...]\fP]
[\fB/framerate:\fP\fI<1-120>\fP]
[\fB/gfx-stats-period:\fP\fI<seconds>\fP]
[\fB/codec-first:\fP\fIhardware[:<id>,...]|software\fP]
[\fB/codec-list\fP]
[\fB/stats-ipc\fP[:\fI<path>|on|off\fP]]
[\fB+auth\fP]
[\fB-may-view\fP]
[\fB-may-interact\fP]
[\fB/sec:\fP\fI<rdp|tls|nla|ext>\fP]
[\fB-sec-rdp\fP]
[\fB-sec-tls\fP]
[\fB-sec-nla\fP]
[\fB-sec-ext\fP]
[\fB/sam-file:\fP\fI<file>\fP]
[\fB/version\fP]
[\fB/help\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B @MANPAGE_NAME@
can be used to share a running X display like with VNC but by using the RDP
instead. It is also possibly to share only parts (rect) of the display.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP /ipc-socket:<ipc-socket>
If this option is set an ipc socket with the path \fIipc-socket\fP is used
instead of a TCP socket.
.IP /port:<port>
Set the port to use. Default is 3389.
This option is ignored if ipc-socket is used.
.IP /monitors:<1,2,3,...>
Select the monitor(s) to share.
.IP /subsystem:<name>[,<name>...]
Try only these subsystems, in this order (the default is all that are built in, in
the order of the build). The first one that finds a monitor is used, so this
selects one, e.g. /subsystem:X11. Without a value the subsystems are listed with
the monitors each finds; * marks the one that would be used.
.IP /rect:<x,y,w,h>      
Select rectangle within monitor to share.
.IP /codec:<codec>:<key>:<value>[,<codec>:<key>:<value>...]
The encoders' settings, for the codecs built in (bit rates in kbit/s):
h264: rate-control:vbr|cqp, bitrate, qp:<0-51>;
av1: rate-control:vbr|cbr|cq|q, bitrate, cq-level:<0-63>, threads:<0-64>, cpu-used:<0-12>,
tile-columns:<0-6>;
av2: rate-control:vbr|cbr|cq|q, bitrate, qp:<0-255>, threads:<1-64>, cpu-used:<0-10>,
tile-columns:<0-6>, dpb-size:<1-16>;
hevc: rate-control:vbr, bitrate. A key it does not name keeps its default; a wrong key or value
is an error and changes nothing. The same value can come from FREERDP_SHADOW_CODEC in the
configuration file and in the environment: the file's keys first, the environment's over them,
the command line's over both; a wrong value there is logged and passed over. E.g.
/codec:av1:rate-control:cq,av1:cq-level:26,av1:bitrate:4000,av1:threads:8.
.IP /framerate:<1-120>
The most frames per second the server captures and sends, for every subsystem
and codec, e.g. /framerate:30. Without it each keeps its own: the encoders go
up to 60, the H.264 encoder is set to 30, the macOS capture asks for 60 and the
GNOME (Mutter) capture for up to 60. A value outside 1 to 120 is an error.
The same value can come from FREERDP_SHADOW_FRAMERATE in the environment or in
the configuration file (what a session manager passes on); the command line
wins over it, and a wrong value there is logged and ignored.
.IP /gfx-stats-period:<seconds>
Every <seconds>, one line per client at INFO on the logger
com.freerdp.server.shadow.gfx: the rounds captured for it, the frames sent,
held and merged, the acknowledgements and their delay, the encode time and the
frames in flight. The server counts them always; without the option (or with 0)
it writes them only when that logger is at DEBUG or TRACE, every 5 seconds,
e.g. WLOG_FILTER=com.freerdp.server.shadow.gfx:DEBUG. The same value can come from
FREERDP_SHADOW_GFX_STATS_PERIOD in the environment or in the configuration file; the
command line wins over it, and a wrong value there is logged and ignored.
.IP /codec-first:hardware[:<id>[,<id>...]]|software
Which video encoder goes first (AV1 4:2:0), the same option as the client's (which picks the
decoder): hardware, the default, encodes on the GPU when one takes the stream: the devices <id>
of /codec-list in this order, or any; libaom takes over when none does or when it fails.
software encodes with libaom. E.g. /codec-first:hardware:1,0. The GPU's encoder (Windows: Media
Foundation) keeps the rate control of /codec: cbr and vbr at the bit rate, cq and q at its quality
100 - cq-level x 5 / 8 (the default cq-level 16: quality 90; 63: 61).
.IP /codec-list
List this machine's hardware video encoders, one per line with the id of /codec-first, e.g.
"0 Media Foundation NVIDIA AV1 Encoder MFT (AV1 4:2:0 encode)", and exit.
.IP /stats-ipc[:<path>|on|off]
The same counters for a program: at the end of each client's period (5 seconds, or
/gfx-stats-period), one line of JSON (schema "freerdp-shadow-stats", version 1)
with the newest period of every client goes to a local stream socket. A reader
gets the newest line at once, then each new one; a slow reader gets the newest
line, never a queue; at most 8 readers. Only the user that runs the server may
connect: the socket is 0600 in a directory nobody else may change, and its
readers' user is checked. Each client carries a random connection_id, which the
client also gets over the stats channel, the frame ids of its period (the ids the
client acknowledges, to join both sides without a clock), its peer address and,
for people, its start time. Without a path, or with on, it is
freerdp-shadow-<pid>.stats.sock in XDG_RUNTIME_DIR or TMPDIR
(on Windows a named pipe, \\\\.\\pipe\\freerdp-shadow-<pid>.stats, and a bare name gets
that prefix); the path is logged at start. Several servers
(a session manager's) need on, not one path: a path that cannot open is logged and
the server runs without the socket. Off by default: no socket, no thread. The same
value can come from FREERDP_SHADOW_STATS_IPC in the environment or in the
configuration file.
.IP -auth
Disable authentication. If authentication is enabled PAM is used with the
X11 subsystem. Running as root is not necessary, however if run as user only
the same user that started @MANPAGE_NAME@ can authenticate.
.br
\fBWarning\fP: If authentication is disabled \fIeveryone\fP can connect.
.IP -may-view
Clients may view without prompt.
.IP -may-interact
Clients may interact without prompt.
.IP /sec:<rdp|tls|nla|ext>
Force a specific protocol security
.IP -sec-rdp
Disable RDP security (default:on)
.IP -sec-tls
Disable TLS protocol security (default:on)
.IP -sec-nla
Disable NLA protocol security (default:on)
.IP -sec-ext
Disable NLA extended protocol security (default:on)
.IP /sam-file:<file>
NTLM SAM file for NLA authentication
.IP /version
Print the version and exit.
.IP /help
Print the help and exit.

.SH USAGE

#MANPAGE_NAME@ - start the shadow server on port 3389 with NLA security, SAM database at /etc/winpr/SAM
.br
@MANPAGE_NAME@ /sam-file:SAM.db - same as above, but a custom SAM database provided as argument
.br
@MANPAGE_NAME@ -sec-nla -sec-ext - start the shadow server on port 3389 with TLS security, NLA and extended NLA off. This allows authenticating against PAM with unix users. Be aware that the password is transmitted plain text like with basic HTTP auth

.SH EXAMPLES
@MANPAGE_NAME@ /port:12345

When run as user within a X session (for example from an xterm) a socket on
12345 is opened and the current display is shared via RDP.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful program execution.
.TP
.B 1
Otherwise.

.SH SEE ALSO
freerdp(7) wlog(7)

.SH AUTHOR
FreeRDP <team@freerdp.com>
